WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.445
Film Courage: What is story therapy?

00:00:01.445 --> 00:00:05.520
Jen Grisanti, Speaker/Story/Career Consultant,&nbsp;
Author: Story Therapy is something that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:14.280
developed with the recognition with how psychology&nbsp;
and story are so connected whether it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:23.280
psychology of the writer the psychology of the&nbsp;
protagonist or the psychology of the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:34.320
it is all about the transformation that happens&nbsp;
so it's therapeutic. Story Therapy was a term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:42.060
that came to me with the recognition. I knew&nbsp;
about narrative therapy that was something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:51.180
that I studied when I was taking a deep dive into&nbsp;
looking at story from all sides. I'm all about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:59.940
idea of how do we draw from meditation? How do we&nbsp;
draw from spiritual books? How do we look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:10.440
healing books that help writers that help us&nbsp;
also take a deep dive into the psychology of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:21.960
character through looking at a wound that didn't&nbsp;
heal in the past that is creating a block in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:32.880
present and preventing the character from hitting&nbsp;
the dream in the future? The recognition of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:44.460
was that through the pursuit if the pursuit&nbsp;
is an opportunity for the character to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:54.300
aware of the flaw that is holding them back and an&nbsp;
opportunity for them to go one step toward healing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:03.840
that earlier wound then we get how the story&nbsp;
is therapy does the viewer also become healed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:14.640
it's interesting we just watched a show&nbsp;
by Abi Morgan called The Split on Hulu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:23.820
and it's interesting that you say that because&nbsp;
when I was an executive at spelling television I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:33.900
went through my parents divorce after 25 years&nbsp;
then I went through a divorce myself so I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:45.420
in this bubble of of pain and of hurt and I&nbsp;
remember going to the president of spelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:52.680
and saying we should do a show on divorce this is&nbsp;
something that would help the audience heal this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:59.220
is something that 50 of the us is going through&nbsp;
and if they're not going through it then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:06.420
their family is going through it or they know it&nbsp;
through their friends so the audience is massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:16.500
and at that point so many shows dealt with divorce&nbsp;
but they did surface level and The Split by Abi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:28.860
Morgan takes a much deeper dive into divorce where&nbsp;
I felt like being in a committed relationship and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:38.340
watching three seasons of The Split was amazing&nbsp;
in that it brought up questions for couples to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:50.460
ask each other by showing through the character&nbsp;
transformation of what if somebody has a secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:59.040
that could get in the way of the marriage and the&nbsp;
idea of through a family law firm you have Hannah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:07.800
who is a sought after divorce attorney and then&nbsp;
you she has two sisters and a single mom that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:16.200
raised them we find in the series Arc that the&nbsp;
father returns after a 30-year absence so you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:22.560
three characters who have the same wound you're&nbsp;
going through the main worldview of Hannah and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:31.080
Hannah has a secret and this secret could shatter&nbsp;
her marriage and the secret also involves a love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:38.340
triangle which is another great thing for writers&nbsp;
to recognize when we look at love triangles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:47.400
and you see that the love triangle makes the&nbsp;
audience die to know what the answer is and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:55.560
know which direction which leads from dilemma&nbsp;
as well that the character is gonna go in so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:05.880
yes I believe that story is therapy that can&nbsp;
heal the audience and for those of us who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:12.960
latchkey kids myself included watching Kramer&nbsp;
Vs. Kramer yes as a young person was definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:19.920
probably I was too young to be watching it&nbsp;
but that was one that you could identify with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.280
if you were in a situation like that and&nbsp;
it stayed with you like Kramer Vs. Kramer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:34.320
stayed with you you forever remembered like I&nbsp;
in my mind immediately go to that scene by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:43.860
toaster where he is unfamiliar in this space with&nbsp;
this child and suddenly has to learn something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:50.640
and you immediately feel the vulnerability and it&nbsp;
helps you understand the transformation and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:59.340
when you're in it that type of pain which could&nbsp;
go into everybody's biggest fear who's ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:08.460
in love is what is the consequence of loving&nbsp;
deeply and that is the idea of the breakup the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:17.580
idea of The Split and and so when you create story&nbsp;
like Kramer Vs. Kramer and and The Split which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:26.220
was shocking to me that I had never heard about&nbsp;
this show and yet this writer Abi Morgan wrote a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:38.220
show called river that I explored that was also&nbsp;
brilliant so it it when I find shows like these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:46.380
I love exposing people to them and telling people&nbsp;
about them so that they can find them because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:57.780
all about right now as an audience member looking&nbsp;
for a story that speaks to us a story that we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:06.180
what the writer is trying to say through the&nbsp;
concept and how the central character feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:13.620
about the concept in terms of the main conflict&nbsp;
what is the creative concept within a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:23.880
the creative concept could go into the intention&nbsp;
of the writer when you look at things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:33.120
the external concept the plot versus the&nbsp;
internal concept having to do with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:41.340
character transformation and what the character&nbsp;
has to learn and the creative concept of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:51.180
does one uniquely affect the other for example&nbsp;
when you look at a show like Ted Lasso I look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.180 --> 00:08:00.780
a show like Ted Lasso and I think oh my gosh the&nbsp;
brilliance of taking a show and grounding it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:12.480
the idea of two people who have to figure out how&nbsp;
to move forward when a marriage doesn't work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:23.520
then you add the external plot of a U.S football&nbsp;
coach being brought over to London to teach I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:32.940
to coach a Premier League soccer team brilliant&nbsp;
because you look at and you go story is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:41.280
come in first you have the agenda of Rebecca who&nbsp;
her motives for bringing Ted Lasso over are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:48.480
antagonistic however seeing the transformation&nbsp;
between their dynamic and two people who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:56.580
parallel life traumas that are going on and then&nbsp;
you add a soccer team where story is coming in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:06.960
then you also add an amazing flaw with optimism&nbsp;
and the idea of someone who is overly optimistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:14.400
getting in the way and then you thrust them&nbsp;
into the British audience where this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:22.260
is considered a wanker because he doesn't have the&nbsp;
skill set and that's where growth happens so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:29.640
goes into the idea of a creative concept where&nbsp;
the external and the end internal are working.

